        <body>
            <div xml:id="d627">

                <pb facs="1268" n="1246" sameAs="frus:frus1946v01/pg1246"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of State Atomic Energy Files</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by a Subcommittee of the
                            Combined Policy Committee to the Committee
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-13">May 13, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Pursuant to the action taken at the April 15, 1946 meeting of the Combined Policy
                    Committee, the undersigned Sub-Committee which was appointed to work out a
                    proposal on the allocation of raw materials has considered the matter and agreed
                    as follows: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>All raw materials received by the United States as of March 31, 1946
                            and subject to allocation by the Combined Policy Committee, and those
                            materials captured in Europe, shall be regarded as allocated to the
                            United States.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>For the remainder of the current year, April 1, 1946 to December 31,
                            1946 the Combined Policy Committee shall allocate 1,350 tons <pb
                                facs="1269" n="1247" xml:id="pg1247"/> of contained U<hi
                                rendition="#subscript">3</hi>O<hi rendition="#subscript">8</hi> to
                            the United Kingdom and 1,350 tons to the United States. The supplies to
                            be allocated to either nation during the remainder of 1946 shall not
                            exceed these amounts. This allocation is to be made without prejudice to
                            establishing a different basis of allocation for subsequent
                            years.</item>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>In addition, the United States will transfer to the United Kingdom
                            under suitable arrangement 50 tons of Mallinckrodt oxide and 15 tons of
                            uranium metal.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Roger Makins</hi>
                            </item>
                            <item>J. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chadwick</hi>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vannevar Bush</hi>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dean Acheson</hi>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leslie R. Groves</hi>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
